# Intent: Games item 1, the engine core. A game is a data pack, not code (docs/design/game-engine-note.md): a table of states (each with an optional random timer), touch regions, drawn shapes (fill, circle, rounded box, ring, text in the shipped font), English and Spanish texts, transitions (state, event enter or timer or tap, a guard, the next state) and actions (set, add, copy, random, elapsed time, divide, sequence add and step, play a shipped tone, commit the score). maipai_games_core.h is plain C99 with no allocation: the pack format (version byte, CRC-32, optional 64 byte Ed25519 signature), the checker every pack passes before it runs (counts, references, ASCII text, touch regions kept inside the screen edges the unit reserves for swipes, a plain-words refusal in English and Spanish for each failure), the signing rule (a pack in the app image is covered by the image signature; one from outside needs a valid signature made with the update key of patch 155, and only a bench build may run an unsigned one), the player (one seeded random generator, a hard step budget of 64 transitions per event so a bad pack cannot spin, a short tone queue, a save request that fires once) and the top-five score table (lower or higher is better, ties keep the older score). The three games (colour memory, reaction time, whack-a-mole) are tables in firmware/games/*.json compiled by scripts/game-pack.py to .mpk files that the build embeds in the app image as flash data (about 3.8 KB in all, read in place, no RAM). Nothing is allocated by this patch at run time.
# Placement: unit (the player, the packs and the scores run on the robot, so a game works with Home down; Home only names, starts and lists them)
# Upstream: none; new MaiPai files. Touches firmware/main/Kconfig.projbuild, firmware/main/CMakeLists.txt and firmware/sdkconfig.core.defaults of this tree.
# Check after a flash: the host test host/tests/test_stackchan_games.py compiles the core with a C compiler and runs it on the three shipped packs with a fixed seed (parse and every refusal, the signing rule, a scripted bot playing each game to its end, the budget, the top-five table, and 4,500 randomly damaged packs that must either be refused or run without leaving their tables). On a unit see patches G02 and G03.
# Kconfig: MAIPAI_GAMES (default y: on in the core preset when the memory gate passes, see docs/design/games-2026-11-16.md) and MAIPAI_GAMES_BENCH (default n, bench only: tap and status tools and unsigned outside packs). The build stops with a plain message when MAIPAI_GAMES is on and the pack files are not found (source scripts/fw-env.sh sets MAIPAI_GAMES_DIR).
diff --git a/firmware/main/CMakeLists.txt b/firmware/main/CMakeLists.txt
--- a/firmware/main/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/firmware/main/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -593,3 +593,17 @@ if(CONFIG_MAIPAI_OFFLINE_PHRASES)
 else()
     message(STATUS "Offline phrases: off (the wake tone only)")
 endif()
+
+# Games (patch G01): the three game packs ride in the app image as flash data (read in place, no RAM). The packs are data
+# files of the MaiPai repo, made by scripts/game-pack.py; `source scripts/fw-env.sh` points MAIPAI_GAMES_DIR at them.
+if(CONFIG_MAIPAI_GAMES)
+    foreach(f memory.mpk reaction.mpk whack.mpk)
+        if(NOT DEFINED ENV{MAIPAI_GAMES_DIR} OR NOT EXISTS "$ENV{MAIPAI_GAMES_DIR}/${f}")
+            message(FATAL_ERROR "CONFIG_MAIPAI_GAMES is on but the game pack ${f} was not found. Run `source scripts/fw-env.sh` from the repo (it sets MAIPAI_GAMES_DIR), or set CONFIG_MAIPAI_GAMES=n.")
+        endif()
+        target_add_binary_data(${COMPONENT_LIB} "$ENV{MAIPAI_GAMES_DIR}/${f}" BINARY)
+    endforeach()
+    message(STATUS "Games: 3 packs from $ENV{MAIPAI_GAMES_DIR}")
+else()
+    message(STATUS "Games: off")
+endif()
